among logicians, a <a class="xref" href="vol18.html#article-TERM">term</a> denoting the first indirect <a class="xref" href="vol12.html#article-MODE">mode</a> of the first <a class="xref" href="vol7.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a> of syllogism. A syllogism in baralipton, is when the two first <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-PROPOSITIONS">propositions</a> are general, and the third particular, the middle term being the subject in the first proposition, and the predicate in the <a class="xref" href="vol17.html#article-SECOND">second</a>. The following is of this kind:

B A. Every evil ought to be feared;
R A. Every violent <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-PASSION">passion</a> is an evil;
L I P. Therefore something that ought to be feared is a violent passion.
